    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L04-ACTIVITY-022",
      "SourceSection": "4.3: Experimenting with Polynomials",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Try combining some polynomials to answer your question. Use the ones given by your teacher or make up your own polynomials. Keep a record of what polynomials you tried, and the results.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l04.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L04-ACTIVITY-023",
      "SourceSection": "4.3: Experimenting with Polynomials",
      "SourceExcerpt": "When you think you have an answer to your question, explain your reasoning using equations, graphs, visuals, calculations, words, or in any way that will help others understand your reasons.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l04.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L04-ACTIVITY-024",
      "SourceSection": "4.3: Experimenting with Polynomials",
      "SourceExcerpt": "If we add two integers, subtract one from the other, or multiply them, the result is another integer. The same thing is true for polynomials: combining polynomials by adding, subtracting, or multiplying will always give us another…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l04.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L04-ACTIVITY-025",
      "SourceSection": "4.3: Experimenting with Polynomials",
      "SourceExcerpt": "For example, we can multiply \\(\\text-x^2 + 4.5\\) and \\(x^3 + 2x + \\sqrt7\\) to see what happens. We’ll need to use the distributive property, and there are a lot of ways to keep track of the results of distribution when we multiply…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l04.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L04-ACTIVITY-026",
      "SourceSection": "4.3: Experimenting with Polynomials",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Then we can find the product by adding all the results we filled in. This diagram tells us that the product is \\(\\text-x^5 + 2.5x^3 - \\sqrt{7}x^2 + 9x + 4.5\\sqrt7\\), which is also a polynomial even though there are square roots as…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l04.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L04-ACTIVITY-027",
      "SourceSection": "4.3: Experimenting with Polynomials",
      "SourceExcerpt": "When thinking about polynomials, it is important to remember exactly what counts as a polynomial. Any sum of terms that all have the same variable, where the variable is only raised to non-negative integer powers, is a polynomial. So some…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l04.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L04-ACTIVITY-028",
      "SourceSection": "4.3: Experimenting with Polynomials",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The degree of a polynomial in \\(x\\) is the highest exponent occuring on \\(x\\) when you write the polynomial out as a sum of non-zero constants times powers of \\(x\\) (with like terms collected).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l04.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L04-ACTIVITY-029",
      "SourceSection": "4.3: Experimenting with Polynomials",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A polynomial function of \\(x\\) is a function given by a sum of terms, each of which is a constant times a whole number power of \\(x\\). The word polynomial is used to refer both to the function and to the expression defining it.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l04.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U02-L04-ACTIVITY-030",
      "SourceSection": "4.3: Experimenting with Polynomials",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A point on the graph of a function that is higher than any of the points around it.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u02-l04.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
